2)Linux system is doing heavy CPU work and is suffering because of frequent context switching.:
   Context switching means the CPU keeps changing from one process/thread to another. Too much switching creates overhead and reduces performance.
  Since hardware cannot be upgraded, we need to improve performance using Linux kernel-level techniques.
   Scheduler tuning
  Kernel modules
  Real-time processing
  Reducing unnecessary context switching
 1. Scheduler tuning
     The Linux CPU scheduler decides which process gets CPU time.For heavy workloads, we can tune scheduling so important processes get appropriate CPU priority.
     Check current scheduling
     Bash     ps -eo pid,ni,pri,cmd
    Increase priority of an important process
    Bash
    sudo renice -n -5 -p PID
    Here, PID is the process ID.
    For example:
    Bash
    sudo renice -n -5 -p 1234
    This gives the process higher priority.

2. CPU Affinity
     We can assign a process to specific CPU cores. This can reduce unnecessary movement of processes between CPUs.
     Check CPU information:
     Bash  (lscpu)
     Run a process on a particular CPU:
    Bash (taskset -c 0 ./program)
    Here, CPU core 0 is used for the program.
3. Real-time scheduling
    Because the question says real-time processing must be supported, Linux real-time scheduling can be used.
 For example:
    Bash ( sudo chrt -f 50 ./program)
 Here:
  chrt → changes scheduling policy.
  -f → First-In-First-Out real-time scheduling.
   50 → real-time priority.
  This gives the process a high scheduling priority.
  Careful: Very high real-time priority can prevent other important processes from getting CPU time, so it should be used carefully.
 4. Kernel modules
    A kernel module is a piece of code that can be added to the Linux kernel to provide or modify functionality without rebuilding the entire kernel.
   Check loaded modules:
  Bash
  lsmod
   Load a module:
  Bash
  sudo modprobe module_name
  Remove a module:
  Bash
  sudo modprobe -r module_name
   For this problem, suitable kernel modules can provide optimized drivers or specialized functionality for the workload.

